# Resource lifecycle

- Resources acquired by an effect or initializer need a defined owner and disposal path: listeners, timers, subscriptions, observers, sockets, and object URLs.
- Cleanup must use the correct handle/reference and tolerate repeated mount/dispose cycles. An AbortSignal-managed listener can be valid without a separate `removeEventListener`.
- Abort obsolete work where supported and check owner/generation before applying results; see `docs/ORG_SCOPED_ASYNC_SAFETY.md`.
- Do not create subscriptions or timers during render. Avoid stale closures and dependencies that recreate a resource unnecessarily.
- Distinguish temporary allocation churn from persistent retention using repeatable heap measurements. A temporary heap spike alone is not evidence of a leak.
- Test the lifecycle outcome when changing ownership; avoid tests that only assert a generic cleanup method was called.
